TI  - Splenic Torsion in a 16-Year-Old Female With a Wandering Spleen: A Case Report.
PG  - e111195
LID - 10.7759/cureus.111195 [doi]
LID - e111195
AB  - Wandering spleen is a rare clinical condition characterized by excessive mobility 
      of the spleen due to laxity or absence of its supporting ligaments. It may 
      present with non-specific abdominal symptoms or an acute abdomen and peritonitis 
      secondary to torsion of the splenic vascular pedicle. In this article, we report 
      the case of a 16-year-old female who presented to the emergency department with 
      acute abdominal pain and was diagnosed with splenic torsion on contrast-enhanced 
      computed tomography. Prompt surgical intervention was undertaken, resulting in a 
      favorable outcome. This case highlights the importance of early recognition and 
      management of this uncommon but potentially life-threatening condition.
