TI  - Intermittent Torsion of a Wandering Spleen: A Case of Recurrent Acute Abdominal 
      Pain.
PG  - e110126
LID - 10.7759/cureus.110126 [doi]
LID - e110126
AB  - Wandering spleen is a rare condition resulting from congenital or acquired laxity 
      of the splenic ligaments, predisposing the organ to abnormal mobility and torsion 
      of its vascular pedicle. We report a case of a patient presenting with recurrent 
      episodes of acute abdominal pain due to intermittent torsion of a wandering 
      spleen, characterized by spontaneous resolution between attacks and diagnostic 
      uncertainty on initial evaluation. Clinical presentation was nonspecific, with 
      episodic left-sided abdominal pain and variable examination findings, while 
      routine laboratory investigations were largely unremarkable. Cross-sectional 
      imaging played a decisive role in diagnosis, demonstrating ectopic splenic 
      location in the lower abdomen with an elongated vascular pedicle and features 
      consistent with intermittent torsion. The patient underwent surgical management 
      with intraoperative confirmation of splenic mobility and vascular compromise 
      without established infarction. Definitive treatment resulted in the resolution 
      of symptoms and no recurrence on follow-up. This case highlights the importance 
      of considering wandering spleen in the differential diagnosis of recurrent acute 
      abdomen, particularly when imaging reveals a mobile or ectopic spleen. Early 
      recognition through appropriate imaging is essential to prevent complications 
      such as splenic infarction and to allow timely surgical intervention.
