TI  - Discontinuous Splenogonadal Fusion: A Case Report.
PG  - e84795
LID - 10.7759/cureus.84795 [doi]
LID - e84795
AB  - Splenogonadal fusion (SGF) is a rare congenital anomaly in which accessory 
      splenic tissue fuses with the testis during gestation. SGF is classified into 
      continuous and discontinuous types, depending on the presence or absence of an 
      anatomical connection between the native spleen and the gonads. Due to its 
      uncommon presentation, SGF can easily mislead clinicians, potentially resulting 
      in unnecessary orchiectomy. In this case report, a 27-year-old male presented 
      with a progressive, painless left scrotal mass for one year, noted on 
      self-examination, and had a history of primary infertility and varicocele. A left 
      orchiectomy was performed, and histopathologic examination confirmed the 
      discontinuous type of SGF.
