IS  - 1930-0433 (Print)
IS  - 1930-0433 (Electronic)
IS  - 1930-0433 (Linking)
VI  - 20
IP  - 4
DP  - 2025 Apr
TI  - Emergency radiological evaluation of wandering spleen volvulus and infarction: A 
      case report and review of literature.
PG  - 2203-2206
LID - 10.1016/j.radcr.2025.01.057 [doi]
AB  - Wandering spleen (WS) is exceedingly rare and is attributed to the absence or 
      maldevelopment of the spleen's anchoring ligaments. The diagnosis of WS in the 
      emergency department poses a particular clinical challenge as the clinical 
      presentation is highly variable from acute abdomen requiring immediate surgery to 
      chronic indolent pain extending over years. Herein, we report the case of a 
      34-year-old nulliparous lady, who presented to the emergency department with left 
      iliac fossa pain associated with vomiting, successfully diagnosed preoperatively 
      using computerized tomography. A splenectomy was performed, and the patient 
      achieved full recovery. Timely use of imaging aids emergency physicians in the 
      early detection of wandering spleen. Early diagnosis allows spleen-preserving 
      strategies and therefore prevents the life-threatening complications of 
      postsplenectomy sepsis.
