TI  - Intraperitoneal hemorrhage due to accessory spleen torsion: Case report.
PG  - 903-908
LID - 10.1016/j.radcr.2024.10.115 [doi]
AB  - Torsion of an accessory spleen is a rare condition, and it is often difficult to 
      diagnose. It frequently presents as an acute abdomen, especially in young 
      individuals. Only several dozen cases have been reported so far, and instances of 
      rupture are even rarer. In this report, we present a case of an adult male who 
      developed intraperitoneal hemorrhage due to torsion of an accessory spleen, 
      presenting with left upper quadrant pain. A preoperative diagnosis was 
      successfully made using contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT), Angiography 
      and magnetic resonance imaging(MRI). When encountering left upper abdominal pain 
      or intraperitoneal hemorrhage, torsion of an accessory spleen should be 
      considered as a differential diagnosis.
