VI  - 2016
DP  - 2016
TI  - A Physician's Nightmare: Fever of Unknown Origin.
PG  - 5437971
LID - 10.1155/2016/5437971 [doi]
LID - 5437971
AB  - Fever of unknown origin (FUO) remains to be a challenge despite advancement in 
      diagnostic technologies and procedures. FUO is considered when fever presents 
      intermittently without an explanation. It has been linked to various etiologies, 
      which makes it difficult to diagnose. We present the case of 18-month-old female 
      with recurrent fever, splenomegaly, abdominal pain, and constipation. The workup 
      for her symptoms revealed wandering spleen. Wandering spleen is a result from 
      excessive laxity or absence of splenic ligaments. The patient underwent 
      splenectomy and was advised to continue on Senna, Miralax, and high fiber diet. 
      Her mother reported that the fever is no longer present and there is marked 
      improvement in her constipation and abdominal pain after splenectomy.
