DP  - 2014 Nov-Dec
TI  - Wandering Spleen- A diagnostic Challenge: Case Report and Review of Literature.
PG  - 57-60
AB  - Wandering spleen or hypermobile spleen results from the elongation or 
      maldevelopment of the spleen's suspensory ligaments. It is a rare clinical entity 
      that mainly affects children. Among adults, it is most commonly found in females 
      of active reproductive age. It may present as an asymptomatic mass in the 
      abdomen, or it may present with intermittent abdominal discomfort because of 
      torsion and spontaneous detorsion of the spleen. We present the case of a 
      37-year-old female who had features of intestinal obstruction with mass per 
      abdomen. Exploratory laparotomy showed an infarcted spleen. A total splenectomy 
      was performed.
