TI  - [Splenic torsion as cause of acute abdomen in children].
PG  - 587-8
AB  - A six-year-old boy was admitted with symptoms consistent with acute appendicitis. 
      Immediately before placement of the first trocar, a large abdominal mass was 
      observed which on imaging was identified as a torsioned spleen. Due to suboptimal 
      reperfusion and risk of reperfusion-mediated morbidity a splenectomy was 
      performed. The recovery was uneventful. The child had a twin brother who on a 
      routine ultrasound examination was found to have a normally positioned spleen. 
      Challenges in diagnosis and treatment of children with splenic torsion is 
      discussed.
