DP  - 2012 Jan-Mar
TI  - Splenic torsion requiring splenectomy six years following laparoscopic Nissen 
      fundoplication.
PG  - 184-8
LID - 10.4293/108680812X13291597716861 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication has become a mainstay in the 
      surgical treatment of gastroesophageal reflux disease, as it has proved to be a 
      durable, well-tolerated procedure. Despite the safety and efficacy associated 
      with this procedure, surgeons performing this advanced laparoscopic surgery 
      should be well versed in the potential intraoperative and postoperative 
      complications. METHODS: A case is presented of a rare complication of splenic 
      torsion following laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication. Diagnostic evaluations and 
      intraoperative findings are discussed. RESULTS: We present an otherwise healthy 
      41-year-old woman who underwent a laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication 6 years 
      earlier at another medical center and presented with worsening chronic left upper 
      quadrant abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with torsion of the splenic vascular 
      pedicle, resulting in heterogenicity of perfusion with associated hematoma 
      requiring open splenectomy. CONCLUSION: Surgeons should be aware of splenic 
      torsion as a potential, albeit rare, complication related to laparoscopic Nissen 
      fundoplication.
