TI  - Volvulus of splenic flexure: an autopsy case.
PG  - 508-10
AB  - OBJECTIVE: The definition of volvulus is an axial twist of a portion of the 
      gastrointestinal tract along its mesentery. The involved bowel is obstructed 
      partially or completely with a variable degree of arterial and venous occlusion. 
      The colon is the most common site for volvulus. The splenic flexure is the least 
      common site of colonic volvulus. Splenic flexure volvulus (SFV) is a very rare 
      cause of colonic obstruction, constituting 1-2 % of colonic volvulus. Mortality 
      rate of the SFV cases is low. CASE REPORT: We experienced a SFV case who was a 
      20-year-old male soldier. The case had come to the state hospital with complaints 
      of severe left abdominal and lumbar pain and a medical history of relapsing 
      urinary infection and nephrolithiasis. The doctor had hospitalized him with the 
      diagnosis of paralytic ileus caused by nephrolithiasis. He had died after 14 
      hours and 35 minutes from hospitalization. Autopsy findings showed out that the 
      death cause was generalized peritonitis due to gangrenous SFV. CONCLUSION: In 
      this paper, we presented this case and discussed its properties in the light of 
      the literature data (Fig. 2, Ref. 7).
